  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What are vocal cords?",
      "a": "The vocal cords, or vocal folds, are two bands of muscle and tissue in the larynx. They close and vibrate as air passes, making the basic sound of your voice, and open wide when you breathe.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the vocal folds",
        "How does your voice work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do you change the pitch of your voice?",
      "a": "Muscles in the larynx change how tight and long the vocal folds are. Tighter folds vibrate faster and make a higher pitch; looser, longer folds make a lower pitch.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the larynx muscles"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the mouth do to the voice?",
      "a": "The throat, mouth and nose act as a resonating chamber that boosts some frequencies. Moving the tongue, jaw and lips changes that shape to make different vowels and consonants.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the throat and mouth"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do voices get deeper at puberty?",
      "a": "Hormones make the larynx grow and the vocal folds become longer and thicker, so they vibrate more slowly. The change is bigger in boys, whose larynx often grows into a visible Adam's apple.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do boys' voices break?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How fast do vocal folds vibrate?",
      "a": "In speech, adult men's vocal folds typically vibrate around 100 to 120 times a second and women's around 200 to 220 times a second, and much faster for high singing.",
      "asks": [
        "How many times a second do vocal cords vibrate?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Typical speaking pitch is about 100 to 120 vibrations per second for adult men and 200 to 220 for adult women.",
    "The vocal folds sit inside the larynx, at the top of the windpipe."
  ],
